  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PY_COMPARISON
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PY_COMPARISON
  language: 1
  conceptTitle: Comparison Operators
  symbol: ==
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PY_04_COMPARISON
  - PY_RUN_09_COMPARISON
  topics:
  - Comparison
  definition: A comparison checks how two values relate to each other. The answer
    is always True or False.
  howItWorks: 'Python''s comparison operators are == (equal to), != (not equal to),
    < (less than), > (greater than), <= (less than or equal to) and >= (greater than
    or equal to). Comparisons are often used in an if statement. Careful: a single
    = stores a value - it does not compare.'
  exampleCode: 'lives = 0

    print(lives == 0)

    print(lives != 0)

    print(lives
    >= 3)'
  exampleExplanation: lives == 0 asks "is lives equal to 0?" - yes, so it prints
    True. lives != 0 asks "is it different from 0?" - no, so False. lives >= 3 asks
    "is it 3 or more?" - no, so False.
  tip: Say == out loud as "is equal to" so you don't mix it up with =.
